// Kiddo School — Alphabet & Letter Sounds (Class 15) · Learning Path lessons.
// One individual flashcard teaching lesson per letter (owner doctrine: Learning
// Path = HOW to learn). Each lesson opens on its own /learn/{slug}/ page: the
// original flashcard on the left, this step-by-step teaching content on the
// right, NO drawing canvas anywhere. Content is written fresh for teaching —
// the library card pages in data-alphabet.mjs (alphabetCardContent) answer
// WHAT/WHY/PRACTISE and their paragraphs are deliberately not copied here.
// Age band: 3–4 years — full parent-child Q&A, tracing, hunting, first sounds.
// Phonics ground rules kept honest: the letter NAME and the letter SOUND stay
// distinct ("m says mmm, not muh"); stop sounds are described clean; I takes
// its name-sound in ice cream; Q brings u along; X owns the ends of words and
// xylophone honestly begins with z. Every "notice" block sticks to what each
// card's alt text verifiably states (big letter, little letter, picture, word).
// Class number: src/curriculum.mjs row 15 — Alphabet & Letter Sounds, Age 3.
export const lpContent={
'apple':{
 title:'Aa — Apple',one:'The first letter of the alphabet with a snack everyone already owns — the apple card is where letters stop being shapes and start being sounds.',
 seoTitle:'Apple Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter A (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: meet A with the apple card — the letter name, the aaaa sound, tracing big A and little a and a bite-the-answer snack game.',
 intro:'This lesson opens the alphabet at the friendliest door: your child names the letter A, hears its aaaa sound inside a word they eat every week, and traces their very first letters.',
 what:'A is the first letter of the alphabet. Big A and little a make the aaaa sound — the one at the start of apple, ant and apron... well, apron comes later; today is the aaaa in apple.',
 mean:'Letters are pictures of sounds, and A is where that idea is born. Your child already owns the word apple; the lesson simply clips a sound and a shape onto something familiar, which is the gentlest possible start.',
 know:'Two things today: the letter is called A, and in apple it says aaaa — the open mouth sound. Big A and little a are the same letter in two sizes, and both can be traced with one finger.',
 why:'Starting with a word your child can taste turns the alphabet from abstract to edible. The name-versus-sound distinction, planted gently now, prevents a year of muddled phonics later.',
 dev:'Letter recognition in two cases, sound isolation (the first sound in a word), finger-tracing pre-writing skills, snack-time turn-taking, and the confidence of starting the alphabet with a win.',
 vocab:'A, apple, sound, name, trace, first',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say both facts slowly: this is the letter A — and this is an apple. Two names for two things on one card.\n2) Make the sound with the mouth wide: aaaa, like the doctor asking you to say ahh. Let your child watch your jaw drop open.\n3) Trace big A with one finger — slide down, slide down, cross the middle — then little a: around and down. Air-tracing counts.\n4) Play the snack game: set out an apple, a banana and a cracker, and ask which one starts with aaaa. The bite is the prize.\n5) Close by hunting: find three things in the room whose names start with the aaaa sound — arm, animal, anything — and give A a wave goodbye.',
 say:'"This is the letter A — hello, A! And this is an apple. A is the letter\u2019s name, like yours. The sound A makes in apple is aaaa — open your mouth wide and try it: aaaa-apple! Now trace the big A for me, and the little a. Lovely. You just started the whole alphabet."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase A and the little lowercase a on the card, then the shiny apple and the word Apple underneath. Trace each letter with one finger — the card is your child\u2019s first tracing sheet.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does A say in apple?" "Can you trace the big A?" "Which snack starts with aaaa?" — four questions, answered with a name, a sound, a finger-slide and a happy bite.',
 childSay:'"A! Aaaa-apple!" Expect ahh-sounds at the dinner table, A-spotted on shop signs and cereal boxes, and air-letters drawn in the bath steam. The snack game will be requested daily — A is a delicious letter.',
 real:'A turns up everywhere: on shop signs, food boxes, the first letter of names. Every spotting is a rerun — name the letter, make the aaaa sound — and snack time is the standing practice ground.',
 play:'Sound basket: put four objects on the table — one starting with aaaa (an apple or a toy ant), three that do not. Your child picks the aaaa one, says aaaa aloud, and earns the right to arrange the rest.',
 check:'Ask tomorrow what A says: "aaaa!" with a wide-open mouth means the sound landed. Tracing a little a in the air on request seals the lesson.',
 tips:'Keep name and sound clearly separate — "A is its name, aaaa is what it says in apple" said once per session is plenty. Two letters a day is the ceiling; one is better, and stopping while it is still fun is the rule.',
 safety:'Snack games need allergy awareness — check for reactions before playing with food, and keep bites sitting-down bites. Nothing else about this card bites back.'},
'ball':{
 title:'Bb — Ball',one:'A lip-pop of a letter with a picture that bounces — the ball card gives B a sound your child can feel in their lips and a game for the hallway.',
 seoTitle:'Ball Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter B (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach B with the ball card — the clean b sound made with lip-pops, tracing big B and little b and a bounce-the-sound game.',
 intro:'This lesson puts B straight into the body: your child pops their lips for the b sound, bounces a ball on every b, and traces their way around the roundest letter pair there is.',
 what:'B is the second letter of the alphabet. Big B and little b make the b sound — a quick pop made by pressing the lips together and letting them spring open.',
 mean:'B is one of the sounds children can literally feel — lips together, pop. Sounds with a body-feeling stick fastest, and B\u2019s feeling comes with a ball attached.',
 know:'The letter is called B; the sound is a clean quick b — say it short, without dragging an uuu behind it. B and d look like mirror twins and get mixed up for years; that is normal, not a problem to fix today.',
 why:'A sound your child can make, feel and bounce is unforgettably physical. And the early B-d mix-up is worth knowing about calmly in advance, so it never becomes a worry for either of you.',
 dev:'Mouth-muscle awareness (lips pop, voice hums), letter recognition in two cases, sound-and-action pairing, throwing-catching gross motor work, and first sound-hunting in everyday words.',
 vocab:'B, ball, bounce, lips, pop, sound',
 teach:'1) Show the card and introduce the pair: this is the letter B, and this is a ball — say both, then bounce an imaginary one.\n2) Make the sound clean: press your lips together and pop them open — b! Short and neat. Try b three times, quieter each time.\n3) Trace big B — stick down first, then two bumps — and little b: a tall stick with one belly. Your child\u2019s finger does the writing.\n4) Bounce and say: every time the ball hits the floor, out comes the b sound. Bounce-catch-b — the sound lands with the ball.\n5) End with a b-hunt around the house: bubble bath, bread, a bear — collect three b things by pointing and naming.',
 say:'"This is the letter B — hello, B! B says b, like a tiny pop. Lips together... pop it open: b! Can you feel your lips do it? Now trace the big B with me — down, bump, bump — and the little b. Every time we bounce the ball, we say b. Ready? Bounce... b!"',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase B and the little lowercase b on the card, then the ball and the word Ball. Ask which letter is taller — B stands on the line, little b hangs from a stick that reaches up.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does B make?" "Can you feel your lips pop?" "What b-words can we find in this room?" — answered with a name, a clean b, a lip-touch and an enthusiastic hunt.',
 childSay:'"B! B-b-b-ball!" Expect lip-pops at strangers\u2019 names, b-sounds attached to every bounced object, and B announced on buses and birthday cards. Bouncing and b may become permanently married. That is the plan working.',
 real:'B starts half of childhood: ball, bath, banana, bubble, bath, bed. Every b-word at home is a chance to make the pop — and buses and bats outside are free sightings all day long.',
 play:'Bounce counting: bounce the ball five times, one b sound per bounce, then let your child bounce while you supply the sounds. Swap roles — being the sound-person is the phonics practice; being the bouncer is the reward.',
 check:'Ask what B says: a quick clean b with lips popping means the sound is honest and installed. A little b traced in the air completes the pair.',
 tips:'If the b gains a trailing uuu (buh), model the short version without correcting — "listen: b!" — and carry on. Keep the bounce game to soft balls and safe floors; the sound survives a missed catch.',
 safety:'Bouncing needs ceiling clearance and no breakables in the arc — hallway or outdoors is best. Balls stay out of faces, and the game ends before frustration turns a phonics lesson into a protest.'},
'cat':{
 title:'Cc — Cat',one:'A soft-click letter with the most famous pet in the alphabet — the cat card gives C its k sound and a prowling game that ends in whispers.',
 seoTitle:'Cat Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter C (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach C with the cat card — the clean k sound, tracing big C and little c and a prowl-and-whisper game for living rooms.',
 intro:'This lesson prowls: your child makes the k click at the back of their mouth, creeps around the room like a cat saying it softly, and traces the letter that is basically a smile.',
 what:'C is the third letter of the alphabet. In cat, it makes the k sound — a quick click at the back of the mouth. C is a letter of two talents: it can also borrow the s sound, but cat keeps it simple.',
 mean:'C teaches children that letters share — the same sound as k, wearing a different costume. Accepting that calmly at three makes phonics feel like a game of twins rather than a list of exceptions.',
 know:'The letter is called C; in cat it says k — a clean click, no uuu attached. K and C make the same sound and that is simply true; a shrug and "letters share sounds" is the whole explanation needed at this age.',
 why:'Cat is a word most three-year-olds already say with feeling. Clipping the k sound onto a beloved animal gives C a friendly face — and the prowl game gives the sound a whole body to live in.',
 dev:'Back-of-mouth sound production, letter recognition in two cases, soft-versus-loud voice control, crawling coordination, and the sharing-sounds idea that prepares later letters.',
 vocab:'C, cat, click, sound, prowl, whisper',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name both: this is the letter C, and this is a cat. Say cat slowly and let the k sound be heard at the front.\n2) Make the k click: the tongue taps the back of the mouth — k! Try k-k-k, like a tick-tock with a cough. Keep it short and clean.\n3) Trace big C — one open curve, like a half-moon — and little c, the same curve smaller. It is the easiest letter to draw in the air.\n4) Prowl: creep around the room on hands and knees, whispering k, k, cat! Whispers make prowling official and keep the game gentle.\n5) End with a k-hunt: find two things that start with the k sound — a toy cat counts, so does a key, a cuddly koala, a kettle.',
 say:'"This is the letter C — hello, C! In cat, C says k: a little click at the back of your mouth. Say it with me — k! Now be a cat with me: creep along the floor, soft and quiet... k, k, cat! And when we stand up, we trace the big C and the little c. Round and open, like a smile."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase C and the little lowercase c, then the cat and the word Cat. Ask which letter looks like it is opening its mouth — C curves open like a cat about to meow.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does C say in cat?" "Can you make the k click?" "What k-words are hiding in this room?" — answered with a name, a click, a tongue-tap and two finds at least.',
 childSay:'"K! K-k-cat!" Expect clicking sounds in the supermarket, cat-prowling at bathtime, and C spotted on mugs and coats. The whisper-prowl may become the household\u2019s official way to cross a room at night.',
 real:'C opens cat, cup, car, cake, cold — most of a day\u2019s vocabulary. Every k-sound word is a chance for the click, and every real cat met is the card coming to life at arm\u2019s length.',
 play:'Cat prowl and freeze: prowl and whisper k sounds until you call "nap time!" — then every cat curls up still. Reverse roles so your child calls the nap; being the caller is the vocabulary reward.',
 check:'Ask what C says in cat: a clean k click means the sound landed true. A traced C curve in the air completes the lesson.',
 tips:'Do not chase the why-does-C-sound-like-K question — "letters like to share sounds" said cheerfully settles it for this year. Keep the prowl to clear floors; knees and table legs negotiate poorly.',
 safety:'Prowling happens on open floor, away from stairs and furniture corners. If a real cat joins the game, gentle-hands rules apply: one soft stroke, no tail-pulling, and walking away when the cat says so.'},
'dog':{
 title:'Dd — Dog',one:'A tongue-tap letter with a bark built in — the dog card turns D into a sound your child can drum, march and woof across the whole house.',
 seoTitle:'Dog Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter D (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach D with the dog card — the clean d tongue-tap, tracing big D and little d and a dog-parade march that woofs in time.',
 intro:'This lesson marches: your child taps the d sound with their tongue, drums it on their knees, joins a barking dog parade, and traces the letter with the big round belly.',
 what:'D is the fourth letter of the alphabet. Big D and little d make the d sound — the tongue taps behind the top teeth once and the word is off: d-d-dog.',
 mean:'D comes with its own sound effect — most children bark long before they read. The lesson builds on that enthusiasm and adds the two things a letter lesson needs: the clean sound and the traced shape.',
 know:'The letter is called D; the sound is a quick tongue-tap: d. Say it short and neat, without an uuu trailing behind. B is the lip-pop, D is the tongue-tap — same family, different tools.',
 why:'A letter that arrives with marching, drumming and woofing turns practice into a parade. And feeling the difference between the lip sound and the tongue sound is real phonemic awareness, earned through play.',
 dev:'Tongue placement awareness, sound-and-action pairing, rhythm and marching, letter recognition in two cases, and pretend play that doubles as rehearsal — every woof is a d-word in costume.',
 vocab:'D, dog, tap, tongue, drum, woof',
 teach:'1) Show the card and greet both: this is the letter D, and this is a dog — say dog and let the d tap at the front of it.\n2) Make the sound: tongue taps behind the top teeth — d! Quick and clean. Now drum it: d-d-d on your knees like a tiny drumroll.\n3) Trace big D — tall stick first, then the big round belly — and little d: a tall stick with a round belly at the bottom.\n4) Start the dog parade: march around the room, one d for every step — d, d, d — with a woof at every corner.\n5) End with the d-hunt: find three things that start with the d sound — a door, a doll, a duck if you have one — and tap each one with the sound.',
 say:'"This is the letter D — hello, D! D says d: your tongue taps behind your teeth, quick like this — d! Feel it tap? Now trace big D with me — tall stick, big belly — and little d. Then we march: d, d, d, and WOOF at the corners. Dogs spell their own letter, honestly."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase D and the little lowercase d on the card, then the dog and the word Dog. Ask which letter has the round belly and which way it faces — D wears its belly on the right.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does D make?" "Where does your tongue tap?" "What d-words can we find on our parade?" — answered with a name, a clean d, a tongue-point and three collected treasures.',
 childSay:'"D! D-d-dog!" Expect knee-drumming at dinner, d-sounds attached to doors and dads, and parades organised at short notice. Woofs may punctuate the alphabet song from now on — accept them as authentic.',
 real:'D is everywhere a child is: dog, daddy, door, duck, down. Each d-word is a tap rehearsal, and every real dog met on a lead is the card walking past on four legs.',
 play:'Drum the sound: tap d-d-d slowly on your knees, then faster, then loud, then whisper-quiet — the sound gets a whole volume range. End with three d-taps on three d-objects you collected.',
 check:'Ask what D says: a quick tongue-tap d means the sound is clean and in place. A big-bellied D traced in the air finishes the lesson.',
 tips:'If d slides into buh-land, model the short tap — "d!" — and let your child copy the length, not hear a lecture. B and d mix-ups coming soon are normal; more marching, less testing.',
 safety:'Parades need clear floor and walking feet — marching is not sprinting. If a real dog joins the family day, greetings happen with a grown-up close: hand out flat, let the dog come to you, and never bother one that is eating or sleeping.'},
'elephant':{
 title:'Ee — Elephant',one:'A giant animal wearing the smallest-looking letter — the elephant card gives E its short eh sound, a trunk-swinging march and a long word that uses the sound twice.',
 seoTitle:'Elephant Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter E (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach E with the elephant card — the short eh sound, tracing big E and little e and a slow-motion elephant march.',
 intro:'This lesson stomps: your child hears the eh sound hiding twice inside one enormous word, swings an arm like a trunk, and traces a letter built from straight lines and shelves.',
 what:'E is the fifth letter of the alphabet. In elephant, it makes its short sound: eh — the sound at the start of egg and elbow. Stretch the word out — eh-leh-fant — and the sound turns up twice.',
 mean:'Elephant is a long word wrapped around a short sound, and children love the contrast: the littlest-looking letter in front of the biggest animal on the card. Contrast like that is a memory hook.',
 know:'The letter is called E; in elephant it says eh — mouth relaxed, barely open, a short flat sound. E has other sounds waiting in later years, but today one honest eh is the whole portion.',
 why:'A word your child can hear the sound in twice doubles the practice inside a single word. And a giant animal makes the smallest letter on the card impossible to ignore.',
 dev:'Short-vowel sound isolation, syllable stomping (three stomps for eh-leh-fant), letter recognition in two cases, whole-body slow movement, and the early idea that one letter can work twice in one word.',
 vocab:'E, elephant, eh, sound, trunk, stomp',
 teach:'1) Show the card and let the size land first: this is the letter E, and this is an elephant — a very big animal with a very small sound.\n2) Make the sound: eh — mouth relaxed, barely open, like the little shrug-sound in eh-oh. Try eh three times, then stretch the word: eh-leh-fant.\n3) Trace big E with one finger — one straight back, then three shelves — and little e: a small curve with a shelf tucked across its middle.\n4) March like an elephant: slow heavy steps, one arm swinging like a trunk, one eh for every stomp. Elephants never hurry, and neither does this letter.\n5) Close with an eh-hunt: find two things whose names start with eh — an egg from the fridge, an elbow, the edge of a rug — and stomp once for each.',
 say:'"This is the letter E — hello, E! And this is an elephant. Listen for the eh hiding inside: eh-leh-fant. Can you hear it twice? Make the sound with me — eh! Now swing your trunk and stomp: eh, eh, elephant. Slow and giant, just like a real one. Then trace the big E for me — down, shelf, shelf, shelf."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase E and the little lowercase e on the card, then the elephant and the word Elephant. Ask which letter holds more shelves — big E stacks three, little e keeps one tucked in its curve.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does E say in elephant?" "How many times can you hear eh in eh-leh-fant?" "Can you take one giant elephant step?" — answered with a name, a short eh, two happy discoveries and one floor-shaking stomp.',
 childSay:'"Eh! Eh-elephant!" Expect trunk-arms at breakfast, eh-sounds offered for eggs and elbows, and very slow crossings of the living room. Elephant may become the preferred way to walk anywhere. The stomping is the lesson working.',
 real:'E waits quietly in a child\u2019s day: egg, elbow, edge, elephant at the zoo, exit signs on doors. Every eh you spot together is a rerun of the card — name it, sound it, stomp on by.',
 play:'Elephant steps: lay three cushions or paper spots on the floor and cross them with slow giant steps, one eh per step. Add a trunk-arm challenge at the far end — pick up a small toy, elephant-style, and carry it back.',
 check:'Ask tomorrow what E says in elephant: a short flat eh means the sound landed. Little e traced in the air — curve first, then shelf — completes the letter.',
 tips:'One sound per letter is the rule at this age: eh is today\u2019s whole serving, and E\u2019s name-sound can wait politely for another year. If the march grows too lively, shrink it to trunk-arms only and keep the feet still.',
 safety:'Elephant marching needs clear floor and slow feet — the whole point is heaviness, not speed. Cushion-hopping stays low and supervised, and stomping pauses whenever anyone smaller than an elephant is underfoot.'},
'fish':{
 title:'Ff — Fish',one:'A quiet gliding letter with a sound made of pure air — the fish card turns F into breath your child can aim at feathers, tissues and anything light enough to dance.',
 seoTitle:'Fish Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter F (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach F with the fish card — the windy fff sound made with top teeth on the lip, tracing big F and little f and a breath game.',
 intro:'This lesson breathes: your child rests top teeth on a bottom lip, lets the air slide out in a clean fff, blows a feather across the table and traces the letter with the tidy shelves.',
 what:'F is the sixth letter of the alphabet. Big F and little f make the f sound — a windy whisper made with the top teeth resting on the bottom lip while the air slides out. No voice, just wind.',
 mean:'F is the first soundless sound in the set: nothing buzzes, only air moves. Feeling that quiet — wind with no voice behind it — is real phonemic awareness your child can hold in one long breath.',
 know:'The letter is called F; the sound is fff, long and soft. If a buzz sneaks in, the voice has switched on and v has borrowed the mouth — quiet air means F is being said correctly.',
 why:'A sound your child can see working in a dancing tissue checks itself: wind moves the tissue, buzzing does not. The fish just supplies the swimming story that keeps everyone playing.',
 dev:'Lip-teeth placement awareness, voice-off versus voice-on discrimination, breath length control, letter recognition in two cases, and the finger isolation needed for tracing — all inside one quiet game.',
 vocab:'F, fish, fff, wind, blow, quiet',
 teach:'1) Show the card and make the introductions: this is the letter F, and this is a fish — the quietest animal in the whole alphabet set.\n2) Make the sound: top teeth rest on the bottom lip and the air slides out — fff. Hold your throat and notice the quiet: no buzz, only wind.\n3) Trace big F — one tall line down, then two shelves across the top — and little f: a tall line with a small hook at the top and one crossbar.\n4) Play the wind game: lay a feather or tissue on the table and move it along with fff puffs — long wind, short wind, then a racing wind.\n5) End with an f-hunt: find three things whose names start with fff — a finger, a foot, a picture of a frog — and blow a little fff at each one.',
 say:'"This is the letter F — hello, F! F says fff, like wind slipping out. Teeth on your lip, then blow: fff. Feel it? No voice, just air — the quietest sound in the alphabet. Now we swim: fff, fff, fish! And trace the big F with me — down, shelf, shelf — like a flagpole with two little flags."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase F and the little lowercase f on the card, then the fish and the word Fish. Ask which letter carries more shelves — big F has two crossbars, little f wears just one.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does F make?" "Can you feel the wind with no buzz in it?" "What f-words can we blow toward in this room?" — answered with a name, an airy fff, a quiet throat and a wandering finger.',
 childSay:'"Fff! Fff-fish!" Expect lip-teeth experiments at dinner, fff-puffs at candles and dandelions, and wind sounds aimed at everything light enough to move. A quietly blowy child is F\u2019s favourite souvenir.',
 real:'F floats through the day: fork, flower, foot, fish fingers, the four on the clock. Every fff is one breath long — spot it, say it, and let the air do the teaching.',
 play:'Feather race: two players blow a feather or a small paper ball across the table toward each other, every push released as fff. Longest wind wins, not loudest — and the fish card referees from the side.',
 check:'Ask what F says: a long soft fff with the teeth finding the lip means the sound landed. A throat-check with no buzz seals it — quiet air, correct letter.',
 tips:'If fff buzzes into vvv, hold your own throat and compare out loud — the quiet one is F — then keep playing; the distinction arrives through feeling, not correction. Wind games stay gentle and well away from eyes.',
 safety:'Feathers, tissues and small paper balls stay out of mouths and away from eyes — blowing only, never sucking. Keep puff-play off tables with breakables, and park the game before anyone gets too breathless to laugh.'},
'grapes':{
 title:'Gg — Grapes',one:'A snack-time letter with a bunch of practice attached — the grapes card gives G its hard guh sound and turns fruit counting into phonics that repeats itself daily.',
 seoTitle:'Grapes Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter G (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach G with the grapes card — the hard guh sound at the back of the mouth, tracing big G and little g and a counting game.',
 intro:'This lesson snacks its way through the letter: your child makes the guh sound at the back of their mouth, counts grapes one guh at a time, and traces a letter with a tail.',
 what:'G is the seventh letter of the alphabet. In grapes, it makes its hard sound: guh — the back-of-the-mouth sound at the start of goose and giggle. The throat does the work; the lips stay out of it.',
 mean:'G is a snack-table word, which means the practice renews itself several times a day without a lesson plan in sight. Letters attached to food get more rehearsal than any worksheet could buy.',
 know:'The letter is called G; in grapes it says guh — short and clean, made at the back. G owns a second sound, the j in giant, but grapes keeps today simple: one fruit, one sound.',
 why:'Snacks arrive daily and grapes rarely miss a week, so this card earns more repetitions than almost any other in the set. Frequency, not cleverness, is what makes a letter sound stick.',
 dev:'Back-of-mouth sound production, one-to-one counting paired with sound, letter recognition in two cases, pincer-grip snack handling, and the patience of waiting for a counted treat.',
 vocab:'G, grapes, guh, count, snack, bunch',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name the pair: this is the letter G, and this is a bunch of grapes — say grapes slowly and let guh stand up at the front.\n2) Make the sound at the back of your mouth: guh, like a gentle gulp. Try guh three times, then guh-grapes, feeling the throat do the work.\n3) Trace big G — a round curve that grows a small tail at the end — and little g: a circle with a tail that dangles below the line.\n4) Count grapes onto a little plate: one guh, two guh, three guh — one sound per grape, then eat the counting one grape at a time.\n5) End with a guh-hunt around the room: a toy goose, a giggle on demand, somebody\u2019s grandpa in a photo — collect three and give each a clean guh.',
 say:'"This is the letter G — hello, G! G says guh, right at the back of your mouth, like a tiny gulp. Say it with me — guh, guh, grapes! Now count the plate with me: one guh, two guh, three guh... and eat the counting! Then trace the big G — round and round, with a little tail at the end."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase G and the little lowercase g on the card, then the bunch of grapes and the word Grapes. Ask which letter grew a tail — big G\u2019s curve ends in a small hook, little g\u2019s tail hangs below.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does G say in grapes?" "Where do you feel the sound — front or back of your mouth?" "How many grapes shall we count next?" — answered with a name, a back-of-mouth guh, a hand on the throat and a number.',
 childSay:'"Guh! Guh-grapes!" Expect grape-counting requests at snack time, guh-sounds offered to grandparents and garden hoses, and tails added to every circle your child draws. G is the letter that eats its homework.',
 real:'G grows through daily life: grapes, goose, giggle, gate, grandma. Every guh at the table or the garden gate is the card repeating itself — and snack counters never seem to tire of it.',
 play:'Grape counting shop: your child arranges six grape halves on a plate as the shopkeeper, you order one guh, two guh, three guh — and only sound-perfect counting gets the order filled. Swap roles; the shopkeeper may demand payment in guh-sounds.',
 check:'Ask what G says in grapes: a clean back-of-mouth guh means the hard sound landed. A little g traced in the air — circle first, dangling tail second — completes the lesson.',
 tips:'G\u2019s j-sound in giant or giraffe can stay a mystery for another year — if it comes up, "G has two costumes; today it wears the grape one" settles it cheerfully. Counted snacks end when the plate is empty, not before.',
 safety:'Whole grapes are a choking risk for under-fours — halve them lengthways before any counting game, and keep eating sitting-down. Washed hands, washed grapes, and the game pauses until every mouth is empty.'},
'house':{
 title:'Hh — House',one:'A breathy sigh of a letter with a home attached — the house card turns H into warm air on your palm, a dancing tissue and an h-hunt through every room you own.',
 seoTitle:'House Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter H (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach H with the house card — the breathy hhh sound like fogging a window, tracing big H and little h and a tissue-dance game.',
 intro:'This lesson breathes out: your child fogs an imaginary window, makes a tissue dance with a warm hhh, and traces the letter that looks like a ladder between two walls.',
 what:'H is the eighth letter of the alphabet. Big H and little h make the h sound — a warm breathy sigh, like fogging a window or warming cold hands. Open mouth, warm air, nothing else.',
 mean:'H is nearly invisible in fast speech — children hear ouse more than they hear the h. This lesson slows the breath down until the sound is something your child can feel on a palm and see in a tissue.',
 know:'The letter is called H; the sound is hhh — pure warm air, no voice, no tap. The mouth stays open and the breath comes out, which is exactly why the sound can disappear when words speed up.',
 why:'A sound your child can see moving a tissue teaches itself: the tissue dances or it does not. And house is already one of the most-rehearsed words in any small child\u2019s day.',
 dev:'Breath control and direction, voice-off airflow awareness, letter recognition in two cases, fine finger work for tissue handling, and word-start listening — catching the h at the front of spoken words.',
 vocab:'H, house, hhh, breath, warm, door',
 teach:'1) Show the card and open the door: this is the letter H, and this is a house — the house your h sound lives in.\n2) Make the sound: breathe out slowly and warmly onto your palm — hhh — the way you would fog a window. Try it three times; the palm should feel warm each time.\n3) Trace big H — two tall walls and one ladder rung between them — and little h: a tall wall that drops into a small hump.\n4) Play the tissue dance: hold a tissue up to your lips, breathe a warm hhh and watch it dance. Then hhh-house, hhh-hat, hhh-hand — three warm breaths around the room.\n5) End with an h-hunt: find three things that start with the breathy sound — a hand, a hat, a head — and breathe a little hhh at each.',
 say:'"This is the letter H — hello, H! H says hhh — a warm breath, like fogging up a window. Breathe on your hand: can you feel the warmth? That is the whole sound. Now make the tissue dance for me — hhh, hhh, house! Then trace the big H: two tall walls and a ladder between."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase H and the little lowercase h on the card, then the house and the word House. Ask which letter looks like a ladder — big H keeps its rung high up, little h melts its wall into a hump.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does H make?" "Can you warm my hand with your hhh?" "What h-words live in this room?" — answered with a name, a warm sigh, a surprised palm and a small expedition.',
 childSay:'"Hhh! Hhh-house!" Expect window-fogging on every cold pane, warm breaths blown onto grown-up hands, and h-sounds offered to hats, horses and hair. The tissue dance will be requested at every meal.',
 real:'H hides at the start of half a morning: house, hand, hair, hat, hurry up. Each whispered hhh on the way out the door is the card breathing along with your family.',
 play:'Warm-breath mirror: press palms together, take turns saying hhh onto each other\u2019s skin and guess whose breath was warmer. Then aim a joint hhh at a tissue until it slides off the table — teamwork, one warm breath at a time.',
 check:'Ask what H says: a warm audible sigh on an open mouth means the sound landed — the classic miss is silence, so listen for real moving air. A little h traced in the air completes the pair.',
 tips:'If the h vanishes in fast speech — ouse instead of house — slow the word right down at the front door and let the breath be heard. Exaggeration is the fix; correction is not.',
 safety:'Tissues and light objects for breath games stay out of mouths and away from eyes. Window-fogging needs a grown-up nearby so nobody leans on glass, and breath games pause when anyone has a cough.'},
'ice-cream':{
 title:'Ii — Ice Cream',one:'The treat-letter card where I gets honest about its two sounds — in ice cream the letter says its own name, and your child gets to scoop that fact like dessert.',
 seoTitle:'Ice Cream Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter I (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach I with the ice cream card — the letter that says its own name, tracing big I and little i and a pretend scoop shop.',
 intro:'This lesson scoops: your child meets the letter that says its own name out loud, runs an ice cream shop with pom-pom scoops, and traces a letter built like a shelf with a stand.',
 what:'I is the ninth letter of the alphabet. In ice cream, it says its own name: ii — exactly the sound from the alphabet song. I owns two sounds; on this card the long name-sound takes the cone.',
 mean:'I is the first letter whose sound is its own name, which makes it oddly easy: your child already says ii every time they sing the alphabet. The card just parks that sound on a dessert.',
 know:'The letter is called I, and in ice cream it also says ii — the same thing twice. Later words will use a short i, like insect; on this card the name-sound wins the treat, and that is honest.',
 why:'Vowels bend, and meeting that idea through a beloved treat keeps it friendly: no rule-memorising, just a letter that sometimes says its name and an ice cream that always says summer.',
 dev:'Vowel flexibility met early and gently, letter-name and letter-sound held side by side, letter recognition in two cases, pretend-shop turn-taking, and scoop counting for early maths.',
 vocab:'I, ice cream, name, sound, scoop, sweet',
 teach:'1) Show the card and set up the surprise: this is the letter I, and this is ice cream — and here I says its own name, which almost no letter gets to do.\n2) Sing the sound: ii — the note I takes in the alphabet song. Hold it like a long note: iii. Then park it at the front of the word: ii-ice cream.\n3) Trace big I — one tall stand with a shelf above and a shelf below — and little i: a short stick with a dot floating over it like a cherry.\n4) Open the ice cream shop: cups, pom-poms or balls for scoops. Every order is called with the sound — ii-ice cream, please! — before any scooping happens.\n5) Close with an ii-hunt: find things whose names start with the name-sound — ice from the freezer, an iron if one lives here, an island on a map — and give each a pretend lick.',
 say:'"This is the letter I — hello, I! Listen carefully: in ice cream, I says its own name — ii! Just like in the song. Say it tall and long: iii-ice cream. Now scoop for me — one pom-pom, two pom-pom — and hand it over with an enormous ii! What a treat of a letter."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase I and the little lowercase i on the card, then the ice cream cone and the word Ice cream. Ask which letter wears a dot — little i carries one above it, like a cherry on top.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does I say in ice cream?" "Does the letter say its own name here?" "What flavour shall we scoop next?" — answered with a name, a long ii, a delighted yes and an order.',
 childSay:'"Ii! Ii-ice cream!" Expect shop orders shouted across the kitchen, ii-sounds offered to ice cubes and ironing boards, and alphabet-song moments where the I gets a solo. The dot on little i may be explained at length to anyone who stands still.',
 real:'I turns up chilled: ice cubes in drinks, ice lollies in the freezer, icing on a cake. Every ii is the name-sound saying hello — spot it together and the alphabet song gains a meaning.',
 play:'Scoop shop: three cups, a pom-pom pile and one rule — every order must begin with a long ii before any scooping. Swap customer and shopkeeper; the customer pays in sounds, the shopkeeper invents flavours.',
 check:'Ask what I says in ice cream: a long name-sound ii means the honest vowel fact landed. A little i traced in the air — stick first, dot second — completes the treat.',
 tips:'Keep both facts light: "I has two sounds; today it says its own name" is the whole explanation, and short i can wait for insects. Never quiz the distinction — the alphabet song teaches it for free.',
 safety:'Scooping stays pretend — pom-poms and balls are not food and small parts stay away from little siblings. If real ice cream joins the lesson, check for allergies first and keep eating sitting down.'},
'juice':{
 title:'Jj — Juice',one:'A breakfast-table letter with a sound that jumps — the juice card gives J its quick juh, a two-cup cheers game and a daily rehearsal no other card can promise.',
 seoTitle:'Juice Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter J (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach J with the juice card — the jumping juh sound, tracing big J and little j and a cheers-game for two little cups.',
 intro:'This lesson toasts: your child makes the juh sound that hops off the tongue, clinks two little cups on every juh, and traces a letter with a hook like a fisherman\u2019s.',
 what:'J is the tenth letter of the alphabet. Big J and little j make the j sound — a quick jumping juh, like the start of jump and jam. The voice hops once and the word is away.',
 mean:'Juice is a requested-by-name word in most households, which hands this card a rehearsal slot at breakfast every day. Letters that live inside routines need almost no extra teaching.',
 know:'The letter is called J; the sound is a quick juh — say it short, with no uuu dragged along behind. J is a young letter with a short history and one clear job your child can master today.',
 why:'A sound that jumps pairs perfectly with a toast game: clink, juh, sip. Rhythm plus routine plus refreshment is the strongest trio in the set — breakfast does the revising for you.',
 dev:'Quick-release sound production, turn-taking with cups, letter recognition in two cases, gentle pouring and clinking coordination, and the routine-attachment that makes practice happen by itself.',
 vocab:'J, juice, juh, jump, cup, cheers',
 teach:'1) Show the card at breakfast if you can: this is the letter J, and this is juice — the word your child already orders by name.\n2) Make the sound: juh, quick and springy, like the start of jump. Say juh-juh-jump and let the sound hop each time.\n3) Trace big J — a tall line that hooks to the left at the bottom — and little j: a shorter line with its hook and a dot floating above.\n4) Pour two small cups and toast: clink on the juh — juh, juh, juice! — then both take a sip. Three toasts is a full round.\n5) End with a juh-hunt: find three j-things — a jacket on its hook, a jar lid, a toy jeep — and hop once for each.',
 say:'"This is the letter J — hello, J! J says juh — a quick jump of a sound, like the start of jump. Say it with me: juh, juh, juice! Now pour the cups, clink them on the juh, and drink. Cheers! You just toasted the whole letter."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase J and the little lowercase j on the card, then the juice box and the word Juice. Ask which letter wears a dot — little j carries one above its hook, big J goes bareheaded.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does J make?" "Can you hop on the juh?" "What j-words can we find before breakfast ends?" — answered with a name, a springy juh, one hop and a proud point.',
 childSay:'"Juh! Juh-juice!" Expect toast-clinking at every meal, juh-sounds offered to jackets and jelly, and hops inserted mid-word for emphasis. Juice requests may officially become juh requests. Accept the upgrade.',
 real:'J pours itself daily: juice at breakfast, jam on toast, jump in the garden, jelly at parties. Each juh at the table is the card repeating its lesson while everyone is busy drinking.',
 play:'Cups up: two small cups, one rule — nobody drinks until the toast is said, and the toast must start with a clean juh. Vary the volume: giant juh, tiny juh, whisper juh. The silliest toast wins the first sip.',
 check:'Ask what J says: a quick springy juh with no trailing uuu means the sound landed. A little j traced in the air — hook first, dot second — finishes the lesson.',
 tips:'Keep the juh short and the toasts many — sound length is the whole lesson here. If breakfast runs late, the cups can be empty; the clink and the sound are the point, not the drink.',
 safety:'Pouring stays small and supervised — a splash of water works as well as juice. Cups are unbreakable, clinking stays gentle, sipping waits until everyone is seated, and no one runs, jumps or juggles with cups.'},
'kite':{
 title:'Kk — Kite',one:'A windy letter with running built in — the kite card gives K its clean kuh click, a garden dash with arms up like string and a twin-letter idea to shrug at happily.',
 seoTitle:'Kite Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter K (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach K with the kite card — the clean kuh sound shared with C, tracing big K and little k and a run-like-a-kite game.',
 intro:'This lesson runs: your child taps the k sound at the back of their mouth, sprints with arms raised like kite string, and meets the letter that shares its sound with C.',
 what:'K is the eleventh letter of the alphabet. It makes the k sound — the same quick click at the back of the mouth that C makes in cat. Two letters, one sound; English shares them around.',
 mean:'K is the second letter to arrive with a sound your child already owns. Meeting that as a game of twins — C and K sharing one sound like friends sharing a bike — keeps it friendly for life.',
 know:'The letter is called K; the sound is kuh, the clean back-of-mouth click. Silent-k words like knife and know are strictly off the menu today — today K flies a kite and that is all.',
 why:'Kites mean wind, running and a grown-up holding the string, so this card unlocks an outing, not just a lesson. And hearing cat\u2019s sound come out of kite plants the sharing idea early and painlessly.',
 dev:'Back-of-mouth click production, sound-sharing awareness, letter recognition in two cases, whole-body sprinting with quick direction changes, and arm-up postural play that doubles as kite pretending.',
 vocab:'K, kite, kuh, wind, run, string',
 teach:'1) Show the card and look up: this is the letter K, and this is a kite — high up, holding on to its string.\n2) Make the click: tongue taps the back of the mouth — kuh! The same sound cat\u2019s C uses. Try kuh three times, then kuh-kite.\n3) Trace big K — one tall line, then a small arm reaching out and a leg kicking away — and little k: a tall line with a small arm and a tucked leg.\n4) Run like a kite: arms up holding invisible string, dashing across the garden or hallway, one kuh for every burst of speed.\n5) End with a k-hunt: find three things that start with the k sound — the toy cat from the C lesson, a key, a picture of a king — and click the sound at each.',
 say:'"This is the letter K — hello, K! K says kuh — the click at the back of your mouth, the very same one C uses in cat. Try it: kuh, kuh, kite! Now be a kite: arms up, string tight, and run! When the wind drops, we trace the big K — tall line, then kick, kick."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase K and the little lowercase k on the card, then the kite and the word Kite. Ask which letters look ready to move — both Ks reach away from their tall line with arms and a leg.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does K make?" "Which other letter makes this exact sound?" "Can you run like a kite for three kuh-steps?" — answered with a name, a click, a proud C and a dash across the grass.',
 childSay:'"Kuh! Kuh-kite!" Expect arm-up sprinting in open spaces, k-sounds offered to keys and kicks, and C-K friendships declared over breakfast. The toy cat may be promoted to honorary K. The sharing idea has landed.',
 real:'K clicks quietly through the day: key, kick, kitchen, ketchup, kites at the park. Every click is a rerun — and every shared kuh with a cat or a car seat is the twins idea still working.',
 play:'Kite string dash: tie a light ribbon to a stick and run with it streaming behind, one shouted kuh per straight stretch. When you call wind down, the kite lands in a crouch — then relaunches on the next kuh.',
 check:'Ask what K says: a clean back-of-mouth click means the sound landed — bonus points if your child names C as the sound-sharer. A little k traced in the air completes the pair.',
 tips:'Resist explaining silent-k words — knife and know can wait years. If the C-K question comes up, "letters share sounds like friends share toys" said once, cheerfully, is the whole curriculum for today.',
 safety:'Kite-running needs open space, clear of furniture corners and garden hazards, with shoes on and eyes on the ground rather than the sky. Ribbon games stay far from necks, and real kite string is a grown-up\u2019s job.'},
'lion':{
 title:'Ll — Lion',one:'A tongue-tip letter with the loudest roar in the set — the lion card makes L visible in a mirror, scalable from roar to whisper and impossible to do sitting still.',
 seoTitle:'Lion Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter L (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach L with the lion card — the tongue-tip lll sound you can see in a mirror, tracing big L and little l and a roar-and-whisper game.',
 intro:'This lesson roars: your child lifts their tongue tip for a sound they can actually watch happening, switches between lion-loud and cub-quiet, and traces a letter shaped like a corner.',
 what:'L is the twelfth letter of the alphabet. Big L and little l make the l sound — lll, made by tapping the tongue tip just behind the top teeth. It is the most visible sound in the whole set.',
 mean:'L can be watched: sit side by side with a mirror and the tongue lift is unmistakable. Seeing a sound removes all guesswork — the mouth shows its own homework, and the giggles come free.',
 know:'The letter is called L; the sound is lll — tongue tip up, voice on. Hold it and it lasts as long as breath allows. The roar is optional theatre; the tongue tap is the actual lesson.',
 why:'A sound that can be seen, held and scaled from roar to whisper gives one letter three games. And lion comes with the most requested sound effect in childhood, which this card happily spends.',
 dev:'Tongue-tip placement awareness, mirror self-observation, volume scaling from loud to whisper, letter recognition in two cases, and held-sound breath control that helps every later letter too.',
 vocab:'L, lion, lll, tongue, roar, mirror',
 teach:'1) Show the card and announce the star: this is the letter L, and this is a lion — the loudest helper in the alphabet box.\n2) Make the sound in a mirror: the tongue tip taps the ridge just behind your top teeth — lll. Watch your own tongue lift, then let your child catch theirs doing it.\n3) Trace big L — one tall line down and one short foot across the bottom — and little l: a single tall stick, the simplest letter there is.\n4) Play the three moods: roar like a big lion with a huge lll, whisper like a cub with a tiny one, then yawn like a sleepy lion with a slow one.\n5) End with an l-hunt: find three things whose names start with lll — a lemon, a ladle from the kitchen drawer, a leaf from a plant — and tap the sound onto each.',
 say:'"This is the letter L — hello, L! L says lll — tongue tip up, right behind your teeth. Look in the mirror: can you see your tongue? Hold it — lllll — and let it roar: LLL! Now whisper like a cub... lll. Lovely and loud, then lovely and quiet. That is the whole lion."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase L and the little lowercase l on the card, then the lion and the word Lion. Ask which letter is simpler to draw — little l is one straight stick, big L adds a foot at the bottom.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does L make?" "Can you see your tongue in the mirror?" "Show me lion-loud, then cub-quiet" — answered with a name, a tongue-tip lll, a delighted grin and a volume change.',
 childSay:'"Lll! Lll-lion!" Expect mirror-tongue inspections after every tooth-brushing, roars in corridors and whispers in libraries, and l-sounds offered to lemons and lamps. The three-moods game may run at bedtime indefinitely.',
 real:'L lifts its tongue all day: leaf, lunch, lamp, lip, the big L of lorries passing. Every lll is visible if you look — and every real lion met, at a zoo or in a book, is the card roaring back.',
 play:'Mirror lion: sit side by side at a mirror, open wide and take turns showing your lll tongues — loudest lion, longest lll, silliest lion face. The mirror never lies, and the tongue lift becomes unmistakable in one sitting.',
 check:'Ask what L says: an lll with the tongue tip visibly up means the sound landed — the mirror settles any doubt. A tall little l traced in the air finishes the letter.',
 tips:'If the tongue stays down, model it in the mirror rather than explaining — watching beats telling at this age. Keep roaring to rooms that can take it, and let the whisper version rescue quiet times.',
 safety:'Mirror play happens seated or standing still, never running past glass. Roaring stays at indoor-safe volume near small siblings and sleeping households, and lion pouncing is for cushions, not for pets or people.'},
'moon':{
 title:'Mm — Moon',one:'A humming letter with the night sky attached — the moon card makes M a sound your child can hold forever, spot in the sky and hum at bedtime every single night.',
 seoTitle:'Moon Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter M (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach M with the moon card — the humming mmm sound with lips closed, tracing big M and little m and a sky-spotting game.',
 intro:'This lesson hums: your child closes their lips and holds the mmm for as long as breath allows, hunts the real moon out of the window, and traces a letter full of mountain peaks.',
 what:'M is the thirteenth letter of the alphabet. Big M and little m make the m sound — mmm, a hum made with lips together and the voice switched on. It lasts exactly as long as your breath does.',
 mean:'M is the easiest sound in the set to feel and the hardest to miss: lips closed, hum out. Attach it to the moon and the practice renews itself every clear night, right at the calmest hour.',
 know:'The letter is called M; the sound is mmm — closed lips, humming voice, no burst of air. Hold it long, hold it short, taste something delicious with it: the hum stays the same throughout.',
 why:'A bedtime word means bedtime practice, and no other card lands at a calmer hour. The mmm contest turns breath control into a game, and the moon turns the sky into a flashcard that comes out nightly.',
 dev:'Humming with closed lips, breath-length control, letter recognition in two cases, sky observation and pointing, and the evening-routine attachment that lets this card teach itself after dark.',
 vocab:'M, moon, mmm, hum, lips, night',
 teach:'1) Show the card in the evening if you can: this is the letter M, and this is the moon — the letter\u2019s favourite address in the sky.\n2) Make the sound with lips closed: hum — mmm. Feel the tickle behind your lips. Try one long mmm, then three short ones.\n3) Trace big M — down, peak up, valley down, peak up, down — and little m: one short stick followed by two small humps.\n4) Play the mmm contest: everyone hums with lips closed and the longest hum wins. Then hum through some words — mmm-moon, mmm-mummy, mmm-milk.\n5) End at the window: hunt the moon out of the sky together and greet it with mmm-moon! If there is a crescent up there, trace its curve with one finger.',
 say:'"This is the letter M — hello, M! M says mmm — lips together, and hum like you have tasted something wonderful. Feel your lips buzz? Hold it — mmmm. Now look up: that is the moon. Say it with me — mmm-moon! Good night, moon. Good night, M."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase M and the little lowercase m on the card, then the moon and the word Moon. Ask which letter has more peaks — big M climbs two mountain peaks, little m rolls two gentle humps.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does M make?" "How long can you hold your mmm?" "Can you find the moon from here?" — answered with a name, a closed-lip hum, a breath-length record and a pointing finger.',
 childSay:'"Mmm! Mmm-moon!" Expect humming at every delicious meal, m-sounds offered to mummies and milkshakes, and moon-inspection at every bedtime from now on. The mmm contest may crown a permanent family champion.',
 real:'M hums through the whole day: milk, mummy, more, moon at bedtime. Every closed-lip hum is the card rehearsing itself — and the real moon keeps the appointment every clear night for free.',
 play:'mmm contest: faces together, lips closed, one long hum each — longest breath wins and chooses tonight\u2019s bedtime story. Then hum the word moon together as the lights go down: the calmest phonics game ever devised.',
 check:'Ask what M says: a closed-lip hum that holds and stretches means the sound landed perfectly. A little m traced in the air — stick, hump, hump — completes the evening.',
 tips:'This is the gentlest card in the set — save it for tired evenings. If the hum escapes into a muh with lips apart, close your own lips pointedly and hum; copying your face does the rest without a word of correction.',
 safety:'Moon-spotting happens from a window, balcony or doorway with a grown-up present — never leaning out. Keep the mmm contest to calm volumes after bedtime, and save crescent-drawing for indoors paper.'},
'nest':{
 title:'Nn — Nest',one:'A snug nose-sound with a bird\u2019s bed attached — the nest card makes N a tingle in the nose, a cushion fortress to climb into and a humming twin of M to compare gently.',
 seoTitle:'Nest Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter N (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach N with the nest card — the nnn nose sound, tracing big N and little n and a build-a-nest snuggle game for rainy days.',
 intro:'This lesson settles in: your child feels the nnn buzz in their nose, builds a pretend nest big enough to sit in, and discovers that N and M are humming twins with different lips.',
 what:'N is the fourteenth letter of the alphabet. Big N and little n make the n sound — nnn, lips shut with a gentle tingle buzzing in the nose. The tongue rises to make it; the lips stay closed.',
 mean:'N is a nose sound, which makes it instantly findable: feel the tingle. And nest is a word with a build-it game attached, so this letter comes with furniture included.',
 know:'The letter is called N; the sound is nnn — lips closed, tongue up, nose buzzing. M and n are the humming twins: mmm closes with both lips, nnn hums with only the tongue lifted. One mirror moment settles it.',
 why:'A sound your child can feel in the nose and a word they can physically sit inside makes this card a rainy-day favourite. The M-N comparison, done gently, prevents years of letter confusion.',
 dev:'Nasal sound awareness, tongue-versus-lip discrimination against M, letter recognition in two cases, cushion-and-blanket construction play, and whisper-voice control for indoor nest sitting.',
 vocab:'N, nest, nnn, nose, bird, snug',
 teach:'1) Show the card and introduce the home: this is the letter N, and this is a nest — a bird\u2019s bed, made of sticks and built high in a tree.\n2) Make the sound: lips closed, tongue lifts — nnn. Feel the tingle in your nose. Try nnn three times, then nnn-nest.\n3) Trace big N — one tall line down, a diagonal slide, another tall line — and little n: a short stick with one doorway hump.\n4) Build the nest: cushions and blankets arranged together, then climb inside like a bird settling down and whisper nnn, nnn, nest.\n5) End with an n-hunt: find three n-things — a nut from the kitchen, a toy nail, your own nose — and buzz the sound at each.',
 say:'"This is the letter N — hello, N! N says nnn — lips closed, tongue up, and your nose tingles. Feel it buzzing? Now say it softly: nnn, nnn, nest. Shall we build one? Cushions here, blanket there... climb in, little bird. Nnn-nest. Cosy, isn\u2019t it?"',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase N and the little lowercase n on the card, then the nest and the word Nest. Ask how the letters differ from M — big N has a slide in the middle, and little n wears one hump where m wears two.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does N make?" "Where do you feel the tingle?" "Can you build a nest for your toy bird?" — answered with a name, a nose-buzz, a pointing finger and a cushion arrangement.',
 childSay:'"Nnn! Nnn-nest!" Expect nose-tingling announcements, n-sounds offered to bananas and naps, and cushion nests appearing in the living room without warning. Toy birds may be tucked in nightly. The snuggle is the lesson holding.',
 real:'N hums quietly through the day: nap, nose, noodles, nine, the night-night at bedtime. Every tongue-up hum is the card again — and any real nest spotted on a walk is the word standing in a tree, made of sticks.',
 play:'Nest builders: gather cushions, blankets and a laundry basket into a snug corner nest, climb in and take turns whispering nnn-words — nest, nap, night-night. The quietest bird gets the softest cushion; loud birds relocate politely.',
 check:'Ask what N says: a tongue-up hum with lips closed means the sound landed — check that it is nnn, not mmm, by watching the lips. A little n traced in the air completes the nest.',
 tips:'If N slides into M, do not lecture — sit at a mirror once, hum both sounds and let your child watch which one lifts the tongue. One comparison a week is plenty; mix-ups here are normal and temporary.',
 safety:'Nest-building keeps cushions away from stair edges and never stacks higher than safe climbing. Blankets stay loose enough to breathe easily, and the game includes climbing out on request — no tucked-in birds without consent.'},
'orange':{
 title:'Oo — Orange',one:'A round, peelable letter with a smell worth remembering — the orange card gives O its short oh sound, a circle your mouth already knows how to make and a snack to share.',
 seoTitle:'Orange Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter O (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach O with the orange card — the short round oh sound, tracing big O and little o and a peel-and-count snack game.',
 intro:'This lesson peels: your child rounds their mouth into a little o for the sound, traces circles on the fruit before it opens, and counts segments while the room fills with the smell.',
 what:'O is the fifteenth letter of the alphabet. In orange, it makes its short round sound: oh — the sound at the start of on and octopus. Your mouth makes a little O shape to say it.',
 mean:'O is the letter that draws itself: round the lips, round the fruit, round the letter. A shape repeated three ways in one lesson feels like discovery every single time.',
 know:'The letter is called O; in orange it says oh — short and round, mouth open in a small circle. O has other sounds in other words, but today the orange keeps things simple and peelable.',
 why:'Smell is the strongest memory hook there is, and this card arrives with one built in. Peeling, counting and sharing segments turns the letter into snack time, which is where practice sticks best.',
 dev:'Round-lip sound production, circle tracing in air and on surfaces, letter recognition in two cases, pincer-grip peeling and segment-pulling, and one-to-one counting that ends in sharing.',
 vocab:'O, orange, oh, round, peel, share',
 teach:'1) Show the card and pass the fruit if you have one: this is the letter O, and this is an orange — round, like the letter itself.\n2) Make the sound with a small round mouth: oh. Watch your lips draw a little O in the mirror. Try oh three times, then oh-orange.\n3) Trace big O — one round loop, all the way around — and little o: the same circle, smaller. Draw both in the air, then on the orange\u2019s skin.\n4) Peel together: pull the skin away in pieces, break the fruit into segments and count them out — one oh, two oh, three oh.\n5) End with an oh-hunt: find three round things or oh-words — a ball, a bowl, an octopus toy if one lives here — and trace a circle above each.',
 say:'"This is the letter O — hello, O! O says oh — make your mouth a little circle, like this: oh. Look, your lips just drew the letter! Now smell the orange... peel it with me... count the pieces: oh, oh, oh! Round and sweet, just like the letter."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase O and the little lowercase o on the card, then the orange and the word Orange. Ask what shape both letters make — one closed loop, big and small, the easiest circle in the alphabet.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does O say in orange?" "Can your mouth make the letter\u2019s shape?" "How many segments shall we count this time?" — answered with a name, a round oh, a circle-mouth and a number.',
 childSay:'"Oh! Oh-orange!" Expect circle-mouths in mirrors, oh-sounds offered to onions and octopuses, and segment-counting at every orange until further notice. The smell will summon the lesson from another room. That is the plan working.',
 real:'O rolls through the day: orange at snack time, on and open and over in speech, circles everywhere your child looks. Every round thing is a tracing prompt and every peeled fruit is the card, refreshed.',
 play:'Segment shop: peel an orange, lay the segments in a row and count them aloud with an oh per piece. Then share them out — one for you, one for me — trading every handover with an oh. Maths, phonics and manners in one fruit.',
 check:'Ask what O says in orange: a short round oh with circle lips means the sound landed. A little o traced in the air — one smooth loop, no corners — completes the fruit bowl.',
 tips:'Let the circle do the teaching: lips, fruit, letter, loop drawn in the air — the shape repeats itself without any drilling. If other O sounds come up in books, smile and save them; the orange owns this week.',
 safety:'Peeling starts with grown-up hands for the first cut, then small fingers pull the loosened skin. Segments are cut to suit your child\u2019s chewing stage, and sticky hands get washed before anything else is touched.'},
'pig':{
 title:'Pp — Pig',one:'A puff-of-air letter with the barnyard\u2019s best actor — the pig card gives P its tiny gust of breath, a tissue that jumps on cue and an oink that runs the whole lesson.',
 seoTitle:'Pig Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter P (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach P with the pig card — the puffing puh sound that makes a tissue jump, tracing big P and little p and a trotting oink game.',
 intro:'This lesson puffs: your child releases a tiny explosion of air strong enough to move a tissue, trots around the room with an oink in reserve, and traces a letter shaped like B\u2019s cousin.',
 what:'P is the sixteenth letter of the alphabet. Big P and little p make the p sound — puh, a tiny puff of air that pops out from closed lips. No buzz, just the little gust.',
 mean:'P is B\u2019s twin: same lips, but B buzzes and P only breathes. Children mix the two freely and it costs nothing — the tissue test makes the difference visible whenever anyone is curious.',
 know:'The letter is called P; the sound is puh, a short puff. Say it with a tissue in front of your mouth and the tissue jumps — that little gust is the entire sound, and oinks are optional garnish.',
 why:'A sound your child can see working in a jumping tissue turns correction into observation: it either moved or it did not. And the pig supplies the giggle track that keeps everyone coming back.',
 dev:'Lip-burst airflow control, voice-off versus voice-on discrimination against B, letter recognition in two cases, trotting gross-motor play, and pretend feeding games that grow vocabulary on the side.',
 vocab:'P, pig, puh, puff, tissue, oink',
 teach:'1) Show the card and make the introduction: this is the letter P, and this is a pig — the noisiest dinner guest in the barnyard.\n2) Make the puff: lips together, then let a tiny gust pop out — puh. Hold a tissue in front of your mouth and watch it jump each time.\n3) Trace big P — a tall stick with a belly attached at the top — and little p: the same shape with its belly hanging below the line.\n4) Play the trot: hands and knees or trotting feet, moving through the room saying puh, puh, pig — with an oink at every corner.\n5) End with a p-hunt: find three things that start with the puff sound — a pot, a pencil, a pet picture — and puff the sound at each.',
 say:'"This is the letter P — hello, P! P says puh — a tiny puff of air, like this. Lips together... pop! See the tissue jump? That is the whole sound. Now trot with me: puh, puh, pig! Oink optional but encouraged. Then we trace the big P — tall stick, round belly."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase P and the little lowercase p on the card, then the pig and the word Pig. Ask which letter hung its belly low — big P wears its belly up on the stick, little p lets it dangle below.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does P make?" "Can you make my tissue jump?" "What p-words are trotting around this room?" — answered with a name, a clean puff, a gust of air and a happy hunt.',
 childSay:'"Puh! Puh-pig!" Expect tissue-jumping demonstrations for every visitor, p-sounds offered to pets and puddles, and trots around the kitchen at suppertime. Oinks may punctuate conversations indefinitely. Consider it fluency.',
 real:'P puffs through the day: potty, park, pasta, please, the p of a puddle being jumped. Every tiny gust is the card again — and every real pig met at a farm is the sound effect arriving in person.',
 play:'Tissue tester: everyone holds a tissue at lip height, and the challenge is to make it jump with the smallest possible puh — then the biggest. Small puffs, giant puffs, whisper puffs: the sound gets a whole gust range before snack.',
 check:'Ask what P says: a short puff that moves a tissue means the sound landed with its gust intact. A little p traced in the air — stick first, dangling belly second — completes the barnyard.',
 tips:'If puffs keep turning into buzzes, it is the P-B twin thing and it is fine: hold your throat and let your child discover which one purrs. The trotting game teaches more than any correction ever will.',
 safety:'Trotting needs cleared floor and watchful knees — hands-and-knees pigs must steer around table legs. Tissues stay out of mouths, and the game ends while the giggles are still the friendly kind.'},
'queen':{
 title:'Qq — Queen',one:'The letter that never travels alone — the queen card introduces Q and its best friend u as a package deal, crowns the whole family and parades the kwuh sound through the kitchen.',
 seoTitle:'Queen Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter Q (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach Q with the queen card — the qu kwuh sound where q brings u along, tracing big Q and little q and a paper-crown parade.',
 intro:'This lesson parades: your child learns that q never appears without u at its side, blends their two sounds into one kwuh, and rules a kitchen kingdom from a paper crown.',
 what:'Q is the seventeenth letter of the alphabet. It nearly always arrives with its friend u, and the two together say kwuh — the start of queen, quick and quack. One sound, two letters, a team.',
 mean:'Q is the alphabet\u2019s first famous friendship: q and u holding hands on almost every page. Framing that as a best-friends story turns a spelling fact into something a three-year-old finds charming.',
 know:'The letter is called Q; the sound is kwuh, said by q and u together. Q standing alone is rare and can wait years — on this card the friends travel as a pair and the crown does the rest.',
 why:'A letter with a built-in story and a built-in costume is a gift: crowns get made, parades get held, and kwuh gets rehearsed from the throne. The friendship idea also quietly explains why qu-words never split.',
 dev:'Two-letter sound blending (qu said as one), letter recognition in two cases, craft skills with paper and stickers, royal-wave motor planning, and the social idea that some letters work as teams.',
 vocab:'Q, queen, qu, kwuh, crown, wave',
 teach:'1) Show the card and tell the friendship first: this is the letter Q, and this is a queen — and wherever q goes, u goes too. They are a team.\n2) Make the team sound: kwuh — q and u said together, like the start of quick. Try kwuh three times, then kwuh-queen.\n3) Trace big Q — a round circle with a small tail at the bottom — and little q: a circle with a tail that dangles low, like a wand.\n4) Make paper crowns: decorate, then tape them on. Everyone wears one; royalty must practise the royal wave before parading.\n5) Hold the parade: march through the kitchen saying kwuh, kwuh, queen! and salute your loyal subjects — the chairs, the teddies, the dog if one agrees.',
 say:'"This is the letter Q — hello, Q! And look: u comes too, because q and u are best friends. Together they say kwuh — like the start of quick! Try it: kwuh, kwuh, queen! Now crown yourself, wave like royalty, and let the parade begin. Long live the queen!"',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase Q and the little lowercase q on the card, then the queen and the word Queen. Ask which letter has grown a tail — big Q wears a small one at its bottom, little q lets its tail hang low.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound do q and u make together?" "Which letter is q\u2019s best friend?" "Shall we parade past the chairs again?" — answered with a name, a blended kwuh, a proud u and a royal wave.',
 childSay:'"Kwuh! Kwuh-queen!" Expect crowns worn to breakfast, kwuh-sounds offered to quilts and questions, and parades organised at royal short notice. Q and u may be declared best friends in drawings too. The friendship has been internalised.',
 real:'Q arrives quietly in daily life: quick, quiet, quack at the duck pond, quilt on the bed. Every kwuh is the team sound — and every picture-book queen is the card waving from the page.',
 play:'Royal parade: crowns on, one leader, everyone else as subjects. The parade only advances on kwuh — call the sound, take three royal steps. Change leaders at each chair so the smallest subject also gets to rule.',
 check:'Ask what Q says: a kwuh with both letters mentioned together means the team idea landed. A little q traced in the air — circle, then tail — completes the coronation.',
 tips:'Q alone can wait for school years — never quiz it now. If u gets forgotten, draw the two letters holding hands on paper once; the story does the memory work better than repeating the fact.',
 safety:'Crown-making means grown-up scissors for cutting and tape instead of staples near small heads. Parades need clear floor and no sprinting in socks, and royal waving stays indoors-safe, away from lamps and shelves.'},
'rainbow':{
 title:'Rr — Rainbow',one:'A growly letter with the sky\u2019s best trick attached — the rainbow card gives R a rumble your child can hold, colour bands to draw one rrr at a time and a contest with no loser.',
 seoTitle:'Rainbow Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter R (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach R with the rainbow card — the growly rrr sound you can hold, tracing big R and little r and a draw-a-rainbow game.',
 intro:'This lesson rumbles: your child switches on a growl that lasts all breath long, draws a rainbow one rrr per band, and traces a letter with a leg kicking out to the side.',
 what:'R is the eighteenth letter of the alphabet. Big R and little r make the r sound — rrr, the growl of a friendly dog, the rumble of a small engine. Start it and hold it: the sound rumbles as long as you like.',
 mean:'R is a holdable sound, which makes it measurable: long rrrs, short rrs, growling contests. Children who struggle to hear tiny sounds often find held ones easier — R is their friend.',
 know:'The letter is called R; the sound is rrr, a growl with the voice on. Some tongues curl for it, some do not, and both are correct — the rumble is what counts, not the tongue\u2019s style.',
 why:'Rainbows are headline news at three, so the word arrives pre-loaded with excitement. Drawing the bands one rrr at a time stretches one word into a whole craft session of sound practice.',
 dev:'Sustained-voice sound production, sound-length control, letter recognition in two cases, crayon arc-drawing for pre-writing curves, and colour naming layered onto phonics for a two-in-one lesson.',
 vocab:'R, rainbow, rrr, growl, colours, arc',
 teach:'1) Show the card and point up high: this is the letter R, and this is a rainbow — the sky\u2019s most famous arc.\n2) Make the growl: switch your voice on and rumble — rrrrr, like a friendly dog. Hold it long, then clip it short: rrr, rrr, rainbow.\n3) Trace big R — tall line down, small belly at the top, then a leg kicking out — and little r: a short stick with one small shoulder.\n4) Draw a rainbow together: one rrr per colour band, drawn as arcs across the paper. Three bands is a fine rainbow; seven is the deluxe edition.\n5) End with an r-hunt: find three things that start with the growl — a red crayon, a toy rabbit, somebody\u2019s rosy cheek — and rumble the sound at each.',
 say:'"This is the letter R — hello, R! R says rrr — a growl, like a friendly dog. Switch your voice on and hold it: rrrrrr. Feel the rumble? Now say it with me: rrr, rrr, rainbow! And let us draw one — a growl for every band. What a colourful letter."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase R and the little lowercase r on the card, then the rainbow and the word Rainbow. Ask which letter grew a leg — big R kicks one out from its belly, little r keeps just a shoulder.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does R make?" "How long can you hold your growl?" "Which colour comes next on our rainbow?" — answered with a name, a rumbling rrr, a breath record and a colour choice.',
 childSay:'"Rrr! Rrr-rainbow!" Expect growls at passing dogs and engines, r-sounds offered to running and rabbits, and rainbows requested on every grey day. The growl contest may need a referee. Colouring in becomes sound practice.',
 real:'R rumbles through the day: rain, run, red, round, room. Every growl is the card rehearsing — and every real rainbow spotted from a window or a car seat becomes the letter\u2019s own celebration parade.',
 play:'Growl and draw: move the crayon across the paper only while the growl lasts — rrrrrr keeps the line travelling, silence stops it. One long growl makes one long band. Fill the rainbow together, one held sound per colour.',
 check:'Ask what R says: a growl with the voice switched on and holdable length means the sound landed. A little r traced in the air — stick, then shoulder — completes the arc.',
 tips:'Both tongue versions of rrr are correct, so never correct tongue shape — rumble on. If the growl will not start, try an engine sound first; cars have been teaching this letter for decades.',
 safety:'Crayons stay on paper — walls grow their own rainbows otherwise, which is a different lesson. Growling contests pause for breath-catchers, and rainbow-spotting from windows and car seats stays seated and supervised.'},
'sun':{
 title:'Ss — Sun',one:'A hissing letter with the biggest star in town — the sun card gives S a stretchy sss that lasts as long as breath, smiling rays to point at and a freeze game for the whole family.',
 seoTitle:'Sun Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter S (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach S with the sun card — the stretchy hissing sss sound, tracing big S and little s and a freeze-like-a-sunbeam game.',
 intro:'This lesson stretches: your child hisses a sound that can outlast anyone\u2019s patience, points to the teeth doing the work, becomes a sunbeam on command and traces the curviest letter yet.',
 what:'S is the nineteenth letter of the alphabet. Big S and little s make the s sound — sss, a hiss like a snake or a tyre letting air out gently. The teeth do the work; the breath does the rest.',
 mean:'S is the stretchiest sound in the set: long, short, loud or whispery, one letter with an entire orchestra of practice. And sun is a word children meet in books long before breakfast.',
 know:'The letter is called S; the sound is sss — a clean hiss, voice off, teeth nearly together. Stretch it long or clip it short; either way the throat stays quiet, because a buzzing sss is Z sneaking in.',
 why:'A sound with a built-in contest (who runs out of air first?) and a built-in game (freeze as a sunbeam) fills a whole afternoon. The smiling sun on the card does half the welcoming by itself.',
 dev:'Long-airflow hiss control, teeth placement awareness, voice-off discrimination, letter recognition in two cases, and whole-body stillness on cue — freeze games train the stopping muscle as much as the starting one.',
 vocab:'S, sun, sss, hiss, stretch, freeze',
 teach:'1) Show the card and squint at it: this is the letter S, and this is the sun — smiling, with rays to point at.\n2) Make the hiss: teeth nearly closed, breath sliding out — sssss. Point at your own teeth: they are the players. Try a long sss, then three short ones.\n3) Trace big S — one winding curve from top to bottom, like a snake sliding downhill — and little s: the same wiggle, smaller.\n4) Play sunbeams: stretch your arms out wide, turn slowly while hissing sss-sun, and freeze into a sunny statue the moment you call freeze.\n5) End with an s-hunt: find three things that start with the hiss — a sock, a spoon, a smile — and hiss the sound at each one.',
 say:'"This is the letter S — hello, S! S says sss — a hiss, like a snake in the grass. Teeth together, breath out: sssss. How long can you hold it? Now be a sunbeam — arms out, turn around slowly... sss-sun! Freeze! Lovely and sunny. Then trace the big S: slide down like a snake."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase S and the little lowercase s on the card, then the smiling sun and the word Sun. Ask which letter wiggles more — both Ss wind down the page in curves, one big and one small.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does S make?" "Can your hiss beat mine for length?" "Can you freeze like a sunbeam?" — answered with a name, a clean sss, an epic breath-off and one statue-still sunbeam.',
 childSay:'"Sss! Sss-sun!" Expect hisses at snakes in books, s-sounds offered to socks and sausages, and sunbeam freezing whenever music stops anywhere in the house. The hiss contest may settle sibling disputes. Possibly not peacefully.',
 real:'S hisses through the day: sun on the curtains, soup at lunch, sleep at bedtime, the slide at the park. Every hiss is the card rehearsing — and every sunny morning is the letter arriving in the window.',
 play:'Sss contest: faces close, one long hiss each — first out of air becomes the sun for the next round, arms out wide while everyone hisses at them. Then the sun hisses back. Nobody truly wins; everybody practises.',
 check:'Ask what S says: a long clean hiss with voice off means the sound landed — check the throat stays quiet, because buzzing means z sneaked in. A wiggly little s traced in the air completes the sunshine.',
 tips:'S invites exaggeration — let the hisses be enormous at first, then normal-sized. If a buzz appears, that is Z\u2019s sound visiting; keep your own throat silent and let your child find the quiet version by listening.',
 safety:'Sunbeam spinning happens in cleared space, slow turns only, with breaks before dizziness wins. Real sunshine needs hats and shade; the hissing can move indoors, where the freezing is safer anyway.'},
'turtle':{
 title:'Tt — Turtle',one:'A quick tap with a slow teacher — the turtle card pairs the fastest tongue-tap in the set with the slowest animal, and lets your child practise tuh at maximum turtle pace.',
 seoTitle:'Turtle Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter T (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach T with the turtle card — the quick tongue-tap tuh, tracing big T and little t and a slow-motion turtle race around the room.',
 intro:'This lesson slows down: your child taps a quick tuh behind their teeth, walks the world\u2019s slowest laps of the living room and traces a letter made of two clean lines.',
 what:'T is the twentieth letter of the alphabet. Big T and little t make the t sound — tuh, a quick tap of the tongue on the ridge behind the top teeth. Tap once and the word is away.',
 mean:'T is a speed lesson disguised as a slow one: the sound is a single fast tap, but the turtle insists on walking pace. Fast sound, slow feet — the contrast keeps both facts memorable.',
 know:'The letter is called T; the sound is a short clean tap — tuh, tongue on the ridge, no buzzing. Tap it twice — tuh-tuh — and you can feel the letter working. D is its cousin: same spot, voice on.',
 why:'Slow-motion games are comedy gold at three, and the turtle is their patron saint. A letter that arrives with permission to be extremely slow gets practised far longer than a rushed one ever does.',
 dev:'Tongue-tap precision, voiced-versus-voiceless discrimination, letter recognition in two cases, balance and slow-motion body control, and the patience to finish a full lap without bursting into a trot.',
 vocab:'T, turtle, tuh, tap, slow, steady',
 teach:'1) Show the card and admire the pace: this is the letter T, and this is a turtle — the slowest teacher in the alphabet.\n2) Make the tap: tongue taps the ridge behind your top teeth — tuh. Quick! Tap twice: tuh-tuh. Find the spot with one slow tap first.\n3) Trace big T — one tall line down and one straight arm across the top — and little t: a shorter stick with a crossbar and a small curve at its foot.\n4) Walk like turtles: very slow steps around the room, one tuh per step. Slow-motion races are allowed; real hurrying breaks turtle law.\n5) End with a t-hunt: find three things that start with the tap — a toe, a tooth, a teddy — and tap the sound onto each.',
 say:'"This is the letter T — hello, T! T says tuh — the tongue taps behind the top teeth, quick as this: tuh! Feel the tap? Now be a turtle with me: one slow step... tuh... another slow step... tuh. Slow and steady wins the alphabet. Then trace the big T: tall line, arms out straight."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase T and the little lowercase t on the card, then the turtle and the word Turtle. Ask which letter holds its arms higher — big T spreads a straight line across its very top, little t wears its crossbar lower down.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does T make?" "Where does your tongue tap?" "Can you take one genuinely slow turtle step?" — answered with a name, a crisp tuh, a pointed tongue and a heroic crawl.',
 childSay:'"Tuh! Tuh-turtle!" Expect slow-motion walks everywhere, t-sounds offered to toes and toothbrushes, and turtle law invoked at bedtime. The tap is quick; everything else about this lesson stays slow. That is precisely the charm.',
 real:'T taps through the day: toast, teeth, time for bed, tickle — some of the busiest words in a toddler\u2019s vocabulary. Every tap is the card again, and a real turtle met at a pond is the lesson walking off at its own pace.',
 play:'Turtle race: pick a finish line three metres away and cross it in the slowest steps anyone can manage — first to finish last wins. One tuh per step, wobbling encouraged, trotting disqualifies the whole species.',
 check:'Ask what T says: a quick tongue-tap with no buzz behind it means the sound landed clean. A big-armed T traced in the air — line down, arms across — completes the slow parade.',
 tips:'Keep the tap crisp: tuh, not tuuuh — the turtle is slow, the sound is not. If D and T get confused, tap both slowly and let your child feel which one buzzes; the tongue already knows the spot.',
 safety:'Turtle racing happens on open floor with room to wobble — bare feet or grippy socks on smooth floors. Slow-motion is not hands-free climbing: shells stay on the ground, and laps end before legs do.'},
'umbrella':{
 title:'Uu — Umbrella',one:'The trickiest vowel with its friendliest prop — the umbrella card gives U its short uh sound, an up-and-under jumping game and a letter your child can open on a rainy day.',
 seoTitle:'Umbrella Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter U (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach U with the umbrella card — the short uh sound, tracing big U and little u and an up-and-under body game for rainy days.',
 intro:'This lesson hops: your child finds the sneakiest vowel sound there is, jumps up on uh and ducks low on under, opens an imaginary umbrella and traces the letter shaped like a cup.',
 what:'U is the twenty-first letter of the alphabet. In umbrella, it makes its short sound: uh — like the start of up and under. Say uh-umbrella and shrug your shoulders high on the uh.',
 mean:'U is the hardest vowel to hear at the front of a word, which is exactly why it needs such a friendly prop. The umbrella turns a slippery sound into a thing you can open, hold and duck beneath.',
 know:'The letter is called U; in umbrella it says uh — short and relaxed, the mouth barely doing anything, which is why it hides. If your child offers the letter\u2019s name instead, that is correct information too; the short sound arrives with practice.',
 why:'A vowel that hides needs a body game: springing up on uh and ducking low on under plants the sound in muscle memory. And rainy days become letter days, which is excellent weather planning.',
 dev:'Tricky-vowel sound isolation, sound-and-action pairing with vertical movement, letter recognition in two cases, shoulder shrugs and crouching for gross-motor range, and listening for sounds buried inside words.',
 vocab:'U, umbrella, uh, up, under, rain',
 teach:'1) Show the card and unfold the story: this is the letter U, and this is an umbrella — ready for rain, exactly like its sound is ready to hide.\n2) Make the sound: uh — short, relaxed, the sound at the start of up. Try uh three times, shrugging your shoulders up each time.\n3) Trace big U — down, curve along the bottom, up the other side, like a cup — and little u: the same cup, smaller.\n4) Play the up-under game: spring up on uh, duck low on under, spring up again on umbrella. Your body spells the words while your mouth says them.\n5) End with a uh-hunt: find two things whose names hide the uh sound — one thing up high, one thing hiding under the table — and shrug the sound out of each.',
 say:'"This is the letter U — hello, U! In umbrella, U says uh — short and quick, like the start of up. Say it with a shrug: uh! Now play it with me — spring up on uh, duck down low on under, and stretch up big on umbrella! Then trace the big U: down, round the bottom, and up. A cup of a letter."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase U and the little lowercase u on the card, then the umbrella and the word Umbrella. Ask what shape the letters hold — both Us curve like an open cup, ready to catch the rain.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does U say in umbrella?" "Can you spring up on uh and duck on under?" "What is hiding under the table right now?" — answered with a name, a short uh, one jump, one duck and a discovery.',
 childSay:'"Uh! Uh-umbrella!" Expect shoulder-shrugs mid-word, uh-sounds offered to upside-down things, and umbrella-opening at the first grey cloud. The up-under game may need referee-like fairness. Rain becomes a phonics forecast.',
 real:'U umbrellas itself through the week: up, under, umbrella at the door, underpants at laundry time. Every shrugging uh is the card rehearsing — and every rainy walk is the letter standing open in your child\u2019s hand.',
 play:'Up-under-umbrella: call the words at random — up means a big hop, under means a low duck, umbrella means arms overhead in a dome. Mix the order faster and faster until the giggles win, then finish with one calm cup traced in the air.',
 check:'Ask what U says in umbrella: a short relaxed uh with a shoulder-shrug means the slippery sound landed. A little u traced in the air — one smooth cup — completes the rainy-day kit.',
 tips:'If the letter\u2019s name shows up instead of the sound, accept it warmly — both facts are true — then model uh inside up and under and let the short version creep in through the game. Shrugs help; lectures never do.',
 safety:'Hopping and ducking need cleared floor and soft landings — no umbrella-arms near lamps or shelves. Real umbrellas open with a grown-up\u2019s help, away from eyes, and stay outdoors where the rain is.'},
'van':{
 title:'Vv — Van',one:'A buzzing letter with deliveries to make — the van card gives V an engine hum your child can feel in their throat, a basket of parcels to drive around and a quiet twin in F.',
 seoTitle:'Van Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter V (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach V with the van card — the buzzing vvv sound with teeth on lip, tracing big V and little v and a parcel-delivery driving game.',
 intro:'This lesson delivers: your child switches on a throat-buzz with teeth resting on their lip, loads a laundry basket with parcels and drives the whole house one vvv at a time.',
 what:'V is the twenty-second letter of the alphabet. Big V and little v make the v sound — vvv, made by setting your top teeth against your bottom lip and letting the voice hum. Hold your throat: the engine is running.',
 mean:'V is F\u2019s twin with the motor switched on: same lips, one purrs and one whispers. Children feel that difference in their throat long before they can explain it, which is the perfect order to learn it in.',
 know:'The letter is called V; the sound is vvv — teeth on lip, voice buzzing, no puff. Hold it as long as breath allows; the van never stops idling. F is the quiet twin, one throat-check away.',
 why:'Vans arrive at real doors most weeks, so the word renews itself in the wild. And a sound with a built-in engine invites a whole driving game — buzzing is the fuel and the phonics at once.',
 dev:'Voiced friction sound production, throat-feeling for voice-on versus voice-off, letter recognition in two cases, basket-lugging and route-planning pretend play, and polite door-knocking turn-taking.',
 vocab:'V, van, vvv, buzz, teeth, parcels',
 teach:'1) Show the card and listen: this is the letter V, and this is a van — hear that engine? It is already saying the sound.\n2) Make the buzz: top teeth rest on your bottom lip and the voice hums — vvv. Hold your throat and feel it running. Try a long vvv, then vvv-van.\n3) Trace big V — two lines sliding down to meet at a point — and little v: the same shape, smaller. Both look like a bird\u2019s beak.\n4) Load the van: parcels into a laundry basket, then drive the route — vvv, vvv, van! — around the house with beeps at the corners.\n5) End with a v-hunt: deliver three parcels to three rooms, one vvv per doorstep, and knock politely — vvv is also for visitor.',
 say:'"This is the letter V — hello, V! V says vvv — teeth on your lip, and switch your voice on: vvv. Feel your throat buzzing? That is the engine! Now load the van — vvv, vvv, van! — and drive it to the door. Beep beep! Parcel for the teddy, please."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase V and the little lowercase v on the card, then the van and the word Van. Ask what shape the letters make — two lines sliding down to meet in one sharp point, like a beak.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does V make?" "Can you feel the engine in your throat?" "Where shall we deliver the next parcel?" — answered with a name, a buzzing vvv, a hand on the throat and a route plan.',
 childSay:'"Vvv! Vvv-van!" Expect engine sounds on every delivery day, v-sounds offered to vegetables and violins, and baskets driven through the house at respectable speeds. Parcels may be wrapped in everything. The buzz has been switched on.',
 real:'V vrooms through the week: vans at the door, vegetables at dinner, visits to grandma. Every buzzing vvv is the card idling — and every real van spotted at the lights is the lesson driving past, beep beeping.',
 play:'Parcel run: wrap two or three toys in tea towels, load the basket-van and deliver each one with a long vvv, a beep beep and a polite knock. Swap driver and customer; the customer answers the door saying vvv.',
 check:'Ask what V says: a buzzing vvv with teeth resting on the lip means the sound landed with its engine running. A little v traced in the air — two lines to a point — completes the delivery round.',
 tips:'V and f are the same mouth shape, so compare them at the throat once — the purr is V, the whisper is F — then let the driving game do the rest. Buzz length is free practice: long vvv, short vvv, vvv-van.',
 safety:'Driving games keep baskets on the floor and routes free of stairs and cables. Beeping stays at indoor volume, parcels contain nothing breakable or small enough to swallow, and parking happens before teatime.'},
'whale':{
 title:'Ww — Whale',one:'The roundest lips in the alphabet with the ocean\u2019s giant attached — the whale card gives W a kiss-shaped wuh, a spout game that rises from the floor and a wave shape to trace.',
 seoTitle:'Whale Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter W (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach W with the whale card — the round-lip wuh sound, tracing big W and little w and a rise-and-splash whale spout game.',
 intro:'This lesson surfaces: your child rounds their lips into a little circle for wuh, crouches small and rises like a whale spouting, and traces the letter that looks like two valleys.',
 what:'W is the twenty-third letter of the alphabet. Big W and little w make the w sound — wuh, made by rounding your lips into a small circle, like blowing a kiss and talking at once.',
 mean:'W is the only sound with a lip-shape this round, which makes it visible from across the room: watch for the fish-mouth. The whale spout then gives the sound a whole body to rise through.',
 know:'The letter is called W; the sound is wuh — round lips, quick and smooth. The name looks like double-u, and in several languages it is exactly that; whichever name keeps your child talking about whales is the right one.',
 why:'A rise-from-the-floor game turns the sound into a wave: curl, whooo, spout, splash. And whales are the giants of every animal book, so the card arrives with respect already attached.',
 dev:'Round-lip sound production, breath rising with whole-body movement, letter recognition in two cases, crouch-and-rise core strength, and pretend water play that builds ocean vocabulary alongside.',
 vocab:'W, whale, wuh, round, spout, splash',
 teach:'1) Show the card and dive in: this is the letter W, and this is a whale — the biggest animal the alphabet knows.\n2) Make the sound: round your lips into a small circle — wuh, a gentle kiss with a voice behind it. Try wuh three times, then wuh-whale.\n3) Trace big W — down, up, down, up: two valleys side by side — and little w: the same two valleys, smaller.\n4) Play the spout: curl down small like a whale underwater, rise up slowly saying whooo, then throw your arms up as the water sprays — splash!\n5) End with a w-hunt: find three things that start with the round-lip sound — a window, some water, somebody\u2019s wrist — and round your lips at each.',
 say:'"This is the letter W — hello, W! W says wuh — round your lips into a little circle, then let the sound out: wuh! Kissy-lips with a voice. Now be a whale: curl down small... rise up slow... whooo... and SPLASH, the spout goes up! Then trace the big W: down, up, down, up."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase W and the little lowercase w on the card, then the whale and the word Whale. Ask what shape the letters make — two dips like waves, one after the other, rolling across the card.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does W make?" "Can you show me the round lips?" "How big was your whale spout this time?" — answered with a name, a round wuh, a fish-mouth and two arms full of imaginary water.',
 childSay:'"Wuh! Wuh-whale!" Expect kissy-lips at bath time, w-sounds offered to water and windows, and spout-jumping wherever floor space allows. Rising whales may appear from every crouch. Bath time becomes the best classroom in the house.',
 real:'W washes through the day: water, window, wellington boots, weekend. Every round-lipped wuh is the card surfacing — and every whale in a picture book is the spout game waiting for its next performance.',
 play:'Whale rise: crouch on the floor, rise on a long whooo and sink back down with a splash — five rises is a full whale morning. Add a shallow bowl of real water for fingertip dips only, one drop per spout.',
 check:'Ask what W says: a smooth round-lip wuh means the sound landed — watch for the fish-mouth to be sure. A wiggly little w traced in the air, two valleys deep, completes the ocean.',
 tips:'If the lips stay flat, model the kissy-circle in a mirror without saying anything — the shape teaches itself. Double-u or wuh: let your child use whichever name keeps them talking about whales.',
 safety:'Whale rises need headroom and clear floor — spouting arms steer around lamps and siblings. Real water play stays a shallow fingertip splash with a towel ready, and crouching whales surface before their legs fall asleep.'},
'xylophone':{
 title:'Xx — Xylophone',one:'The shyest, most mysterious letter, played honestly — the xylophone card admits x mostly ends words, lets a z-sound open this one word and still steals the show with two mallets.',
 seoTitle:'Xylophone Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter X (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach X with the xylophone card — the honest letter that ends box and fox, its name ex, tracing big X and little x and a floor-tape crossing.',
 intro:'This lesson crosses over: your child meets the letter that lives at the ends of words, walks a taped X like a tightrope, hunts for things that finish with x and keeps one mystery.',
 what:'X is the twenty-fourth letter of the alphabet. Its honest job is ending words — box, fox, six — where it says a ksss sound at the finish. Xylophone is the most famous X word of all, and the reason is funny: x hardly ever starts words, so this card borrows a word that actually begins with a z sound.',
 mean:'X is the one card that teaches a limitation openly, and children find the honesty delightful: x has a job, it mostly works at the ends, and xylophone is the alphabet\u2019s own magic trick.',
 know:'The letter is called X — said as ex. Its sound is the ksss tail at the end of words like box and fox. Xylophone starts with a z sound, and that mystery needs no solving today; the name and the shape are the whole lesson.',
 why:'X arrives with crossing shapes built in: arms crossed, tape crossed, mallets tapped together. And the end-of-word hunt gives x a game no other letter can copy — x is the finisher.',
 dev:'End-of-word sound listening (a skill no earlier card has asked for), crossed-lines visual tracking, letter recognition in two cases, balance-beam walking on tape, and pride in saying the name ex clearly.',
 vocab:'X, xylophone, ex, box, fox, ends',
 teach:'1) Show the card and introduce the mystery politely: this is the letter X — said ex — and this is a xylophone, the word where a z quietly sits in for x at the front.\n2) Make the honest sound at word-ends: box, fox — hear the ksss tail? Say box, fox, six, clipping the x neatly at each finish.\n3) Trace big X — two lines crossing, like arms folded — and little x: the same cross, smaller. Draw both in the air, then on the floor with tape.\n4) Walk the tape-X like a tightrope: one foot after the other along one strip, then cross to the other. Two crossings is a full concert.\n5) End with an end-of-word hunt: find something that finishes with x — a box lid, a fox picture, a shoebox — and tap it while saying the whole word slowly.',
 say:'"This is the letter X — say its name with me: ex! X is the letter that finishes words: box... fox... Did you hear the ksss at the end? Now walk the crossing tape, arms out like a tightrope walker. And the xylophone? Its first sound is a z — a mystery we keep for another day. Ex-cellent."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase X and the little lowercase x on the card, then the xylophone and the word Xylophone. Ask what shape the letters make — two lines crossed in the middle, like a pair of crossed mallets.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does x make at the end of box?" "Can you walk the crossing tape without wobbling off?" "What else ends with x?" — answered with ex, a clipped ksss, two crossings and a hunted box.',
 childSay:'"Ex! Box ends with x!" Expect x-clipping at the end of every box and fox mentioned, tightrope walks across taped floors, and drum solos on anything mallet-shaped. The z-mystery may be revealed to strangers. Keep it mysterious a little longer.',
 real:'X crosses paths at the ends: a box of tissues, foxes in storybooks, six biscuits, the x on a treasure map. Every clipped ksss is the card finishing its word — and X-marks-the-spot drawings put the letter to work at once.',
 play:'X marks the spot: draw a treasure map with a big X, hide a small treasure somewhere in the room and let your child follow clues to the crossing point. Each clue brings them ex-citingly closer until the treasure is found on the X.',
 check:'Ask what X is called — ex — and what it does at the end of box: a clear ex and a clipped ksss mean both facts landed. A big crossing X traced in the air completes the concert.',
 tips:'The z-sound at the start of xylophone is a known mystery — park it cheerfully rather than explaining phonics history at three. Practise the end-of-word ksss more than the name; the ending is x\u2019s real job and the best preparation.',
 safety:'Tape the X flat with no curled edges to trip on, and keep tightrope walking on non-slip floors in bare feet. Toy mallets stay for the xylophone only — drumming happens on boxes and keys, never on siblings or screens.'},
'yo-yo':{
 title:'Yy — Yo-yo',one:'A down-and-up letter with a toy that never stops — the yo-yo card gives Y its friendly yuh sound and turns your child into the toy: sink down slowly, spring back up, repeat, giggle.',
 seoTitle:'Yo-yo Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter Y (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach Y with the yo-yo card — the friendly yuh sound, tracing big Y and little y and a be-the-yo-yo sink-and-spring game.',
 intro:'This lesson bounces: your child makes the yuh sound with a hand-bounce for every letter, becomes the yo-yo in a sink-and-spring game, and traces the letter that plants one leg on the ground.',
 what:'Y is the twenty-fifth letter of the alphabet. In yo-yo, it makes its friendly starting sound: yuh — like the start of yes and yarn. Say yuh-yo-yo with a little hand-bounce on every yuh.',
 mean:'Y is a two-job letter — it sometimes works as a vowel — but this card does not mention any of that. Here y is pure toy box: down it goes, up it comes, and the sound rides along both ways.',
 know:'The letter is called Y; in yo-yo it says yuh, a quick friendly sound at the front of the mouth. The middle-of-word y in happy and funny borrows another sound entirely — next year\u2019s story, not today\u2019s.',
 why:'A toy every relative owns makes the word instantly familiar, and be-the-yo-yo turns the sound into the game: yuh down, yuh up. The repetition is built into the toy\u2019s whole personality.',
 dev:'Front-of-mouth glide production, sound-and-action synchrony on vertical movement, letter recognition in two cases, squat-and-stand core strength, and the gentle comedy of being a toy on a string.',
 vocab:'Y, yo-yo, yuh, down, up, spring',
 teach:'1) Show the card and wind up: this is the letter Y, and this is a yo-yo — down it goes, up it comes, all day long.\n2) Make the sound: yuh, like the start of yes. Say yuh-yo-yo and give a hand-bounce on every yuh — down, up, down, up.\n3) Trace big Y — two arms meeting in the middle, then one leg straight down to the line — and little y: an arm, a slide and a tail that dips below.\n4) Now be the yo-yo yourself: stand tall, sink all the way down slowly, then spring back up — yuh-yo-yo! Three slow bounces is a complete performance.\n5) End with a yuh-hunt: find three things whose names start with the sound — a toy yak, yesterday\u2019s drawing, a yawn you can demonstrate — and hand-bounce the sound at each.',
 say:'"This is the letter Y — hello, Y! Y says yuh, like the start of yes. Say it with a bounce: yuh-yo-yo! Now be the yo-yo: stand tall... sink all the way down... and spring back up! Yuh! Again! You are the bounciest letter in the alphabet. Then trace the big Y — arms first, then one leg down."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase Y and the little lowercase y on the card, then the yo-yo and the word Yo-yo. Ask which letter reaches the ground — big Y plants one leg on the line, little y lets its tail dangle below it.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What does Y say in yo-yo?" "Can you sink down slowly and spring back up?" "Can you do a pretend yawn for me?" — answered with a name, a bounced yuh, one full sink-and-spring and a yawn on demand.',
 childSay:'"Yuh! Yuh-yo-yo!" Expect sink-and-spring games in every open patch of floor, y-sounds offered to yoghurt and yawns, and hand-bounces added to ordinary sentences. Being a toy is a career now. The alphabet provided.',
 real:'Y yo-yos through the week: yes at full volume, yoghurt at breakfast, the yard, the yellow crayon. Every yuh is the card bouncing — and any real yo-yo found in a drawer is the lesson on a string, ready to perform.',
 play:'Be the yo-yo: a grown-up holds an invisible string and calls the speed — slow sink, fast spring, wobbly middle — while your child plays the toy. Then swap: you be the yo-yo and let your child run the string. Being the caller is the better half.',
 check:'Ask what Y says in yo-yo: a quick friendly yuh with a hand-bounce means the sound landed. A little y traced in the air — arm, slide, dangling tail — completes the toy box.',
 tips:'Keep y\u2019s vowel career entirely off the agenda — happy and funny can wait years. If the sink becomes a drop, slow it right down; the spring is the reward, and slow sinks make better springs.',
 safety:'Yo-yo sinking happens on clear floor with soft landings — no sinking near table corners, and hands stay free to catch balance. Real yo-yos are for older hands at this age; the body version is safer and funnier anyway.'},
'zebra':{
 title:'Zz — Zebra',one:'The grand finale with stripes on — the zebra card closes the alphabet with a buzzing zzz, twenty-six letters behind you and a celebration your child has genuinely earned one stripe at a time.',
 seoTitle:'Zebra Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Letter Z (Class 15)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach Z with the zebra card — the buzzing zzz sound like s with a voice, tracing big Z and little z and a stripe-drawing grand finale.',
 intro:'This lesson finishes in style: your child switches the buzz onto the s sound, draws stripes while zzz-ing, traces the last letter of the alphabet and celebrates twenty-six doors opened.',
 what:'Z is the twenty-sixth and last letter of the alphabet. It makes the z sound — zzz, the same hiss as s with the voice switched on. Hold your throat and compare: s whispers, z hums like a tiny bee.',
 mean:'Z closes the set with a sound your child already owns: they have been hissing sss since the sun card, and z is that hiss with the motor on. Ending by upgrading a known sound is the gentlest finale there is.',
 know:'The letter is called Z; the sound is zzz — voice on, teeth close, a buzz you can hold. Z is also the sideways letter: tip a z onto its back and you have drawn a stripe, which the zebra demonstrates gladly.',
 why:'Finishing the alphabet deserves a fanfare, and stripes give small fingers a road to trace from the letter to the animal and back again. The celebration is real: your child now owns a door for every sound.',
 dev:'Voiced sibilant production (s with the voice switched on), letter recognition across the whole set, stripe-line drawing for controlled strokes, and the happy memory work of recalling favourite letters from A to Z.',
 vocab:'Z, zebra, zzz, buzz, stripes, finish',
 teach:'1) Show the card and announce the finale: this is the letter Z — the very last one — and this is a zebra, dressed for the occasion.\n2) Make the buzz: hiss sss first, then switch your voice on — zzz. Feel your throat purr? Try sss-zzz, sss-zzz, and hear the twins.\n3) Trace big Z — straight across, slide down, straight across, like a zigzag — and little z: the same zigzag, smaller.\n4) Draw stripes: across a paper zebra, or up your own arm with a washable marker — one zzz per stripe. Stripes are only the letter z tipped on its side.\n5) End with the grand tour: say the alphabet together, naming every letter you both met this year, and give Z three final zzz-buzzes for the road.',
 say:'"This is the letter Z — hello, Z! The very last letter! Z says zzz — start with a hiss, sss, then switch your voice on: zzz. Feel it buzz like a tiny bee? Now draw stripes with me — zzz, zzz, zebra! And then... the whole alphabet, from A all the way here. You did it. Twenty-six letters, all yours."',
 notice:'Find the big uppercase Z and the little lowercase z on the card, then the zebra and the word Zebra. Ask what the letters and the animal share — stripes run across the page the same way a z lies sideways.',
 ask:'"What is this letter called?" "What sound does Z make?" "How is zzz different from sss?" "Can you name your favourite letter of all?" — answered with a name, a buzzing zzz, a throat-check answer and a proud choice.',
 childSay:'"Zzz! Zzz-zebra!" Expect buzzes at sleeping cats and bees, z-sounds offered to zips and zooms, and stripes counted on every zebra in every book. The alphabet tour will be requested nightly. Twenty-six doors, all opening.',
 real:'Z buzzes softly through the week: zips on coats, zoom through the park, the z of sleeping sounds at naptime. Every buzz is the card celebrating — and the alphabet itself is now a road your child can walk end to end.',
 play:'Alphabet tour: lay out as many letter cards as you own — or write letters on paper plates — and walk the whole A-to-Z road together, saying each name and sound at every stop. Z\u2019s stop comes last, with three buzzes and a bow.',
 check:'Ask what Z says: a held buzz with the voice switched on means the sound landed — an sss without buzz is still s. A zigzag little z traced in the air closes the alphabet for good.',
 tips:'This is the finish, so let it feel like one: revisit favourite letters, keep the zebra drawing and stop before anyone is tired. The next lessons will find these same letters waiting inside real words — the doors are built; walking them comes next.',
 safety:'Washable markers only for arm-stripes, with a patch check on the skin first and no drawing near eyes or mouths. The alphabet tour needs clear walking space, and the celebration ends with tidying up — even zebras help put the cushions back.'}};
